128 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Living in an age of technology, social media is a big part of many of our lives. Most social media platforms use algorithms to enhance the viewing experience. After a period of time, these algorithms will gather information on things you have viewed and enhance the viewing experience by repeatedly recommending them to you. The more you "like" these posts, the more likely they will show up on your feed; This creates an echo chamber. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
129 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (An echo chamber is an environment where a person only encounters information or opinions that reflect and reinforce their own, and are repeated inside a closed system and isolated from counter-arguments. Echo chambers can create misinformation and twist a person's perspective, making them have difficulty considering other opposing viewpoints. By participating in an echo chamber, people are able to seek out information that reinforces their existing view without encountering opposing views, potentially resulting in confirmation bias which is the tendency to favour information that reinforces existing beliefs. Echo chambers may increase social and political polarisation \(the divergence of attitudes from the centre towards ideological extremes\), which causes separation within communities.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
130 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (While echo chambers can be found anywhere information is exchanged, finding like-minded people and perspectives via social media is much easier. This has made echo chambers far more prominent and easy to fall into. On social media, echo chambers are thought to limit exposure to diverse perspectives, favouring and reinforcing ideologies. Social media sites also have a special kind of echo chamber called a filter bubble; it is a state of intellectual isolation that can result from personalised searches when a website algorithm selectively guesses what information a viewer would like to see based on information about the viewer, such as location, past click-behaviour, and search history. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
131 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Websites will then use those algorithms to primarily show you content similar to what you've already seen. If you receive all your information from algorithms being sent through your phone, it is likely just repeating whatever biases you have, forming a pattern that will lead to you living in your own bubble. Social networks are often accused of encouraging polarisation and widening ideological division. A study in Proceedings of the National Academy of Science \(PNAS\) shows how different platforms foster different levels of segregation in online communities, depending on their feed algorithms and social networking features. Some studies point out echo chambers as an emerging effect of human tendencies, such as selective exposure, contagion \(a process which amplifies the spread of information in a social network\), and group polarisation.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
132 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Recognising echo chambers can be tricky, especially if you're in one. If you ever wonder whether you are stuck in an echo chamber, here are some tips on how to recognise and avoid echo chambers. Firstly, to recognise an echo chamber, you can ask yourself whether multiple perspectives are being voiced, as they tend to only give one perspective on an issue or an opinion. Their viewpoint is also mainly supported by rumours or incomplete evidence and information. Lastly, when facts are presented against their viewpoint, they are disregarded. To avoid falling into echo chambers, you should make a habit of checking multiple sources to ensure that you're getting the complete picture. Try to interact with people who share different views from you, and take the time to discuss new ideas and facts with patience and respect. Most importantly, remember that just because you want something to be true, it doesn't automatically make it a fact.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
133 0 obj
<<
