167 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (With the rise in technological advancements, the video game industry has developed, changed and morphed drastically over the years. Contemporary gaming typically sports fancy high-speed 64-bit computer/ console processors, flamboyant red-blue-green \(RGB\) lighting and stylish see-through computers. In recent years, mobile gaming also sees a surge in popularity among youth, boasting a wide selection of captivating games that appeal to all. However, beneath the covers of these seemingly harmless games, unbeknownst to consumers, lies a manipulative and exploitative profit-driven industry; some games even solely exist to extract as much money as possible from players. In this article, I pull back the shades and expose the predatory monetisation tactics in the gaming industry, which is more of a blatant daylight robbery.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
168 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (At a cursory glance, video game companies monetise their products to players through in-app purchases \(IAPs'\). They achieve this through one of the most commonly adopted frameworks found in all top-grossing video games, the 3 Golden Hs, HOOK, HABIT and HOBBY. This framework essentially manufactures desire in players and gains control over them in the game. Over their playtime in the game, their choices, and most importantly, their finances and spending on IAPs' within the game world. The industry often uses this model to extract the maximum amount of money a person is willing to part with. Even taking advantage of the few that don't know any better or can't help themselves \(i.e. gullible children, chronic addicts or pathological gamblers\). Therefore to maximise profits from IAPs, design choices are heavily based on the 3H model in conjunction with other dirty tactics to 'concoct' these 'cash-grabbing' video games. Well, what other dirty tactics are there, you might ask? One word: Gachas.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
169 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Gashapons are Japanese vending machines that disperse collectables randomly after inserting coins or tokens. Similarly, video games implement the 'gacha' or loot box mechanic where players use in-game currency, either bought from real money or 'grinded' from a player's blood, sweat, and tears, to purchase a 'roll' or pull' giving them a chance to randomly obtain a highly valued virtual item, cosmetic, weapon or character. Some games with this mechanic can include Overwatch 1, Team Fortress 2 and FIFA. The perfidious odds of receiving rare or valuable items are often ridiculously low, hence evoking players to make multiple pulls to get what they want. To illustrate this, the popular tactical first-person shooter game, Counter-Strike: Global Offensive \(CS: GO\) has this mechanic implemented in their loot crate system. If a player wanted to unbox just a regular knife or a glove skin, the odds would be 0.25575% per case. However, to unbox highly valued knives, that would be around 0.02558% chance per case. Similar to slot machines in casinos, this mechanic encourages gambling and rewards players with instant gratification every time money is spent. This, in turn, builds unhealthy spending habits as players become addicted and start to shell out obscene amounts of money before they even begin to notice they are in a 'skinner box'. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
170 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (In addition, players who wish to obtain a specific item can not outright buy them! The huge pay wall, accompanied by a factor of 'luck' makes it close to impossible for players to obtain their desired item. That is if they can cough out huge sums of money or grind for days, months or years. Hence this randomised reward mechanic is malicious to players', and 'gachas' and loot boxes are a tactic of predatory monetisation.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
171 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Another common tactic of predatory monetisation in games is currency distortion, in which games disguise the true cost of virtual items.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
172 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (When it comes to spending real-world money on games' IAPs, most games have a variety of thematically fitting currencies: crystals, coins, gems, points or credits et cetera. Currencies which are all designed intentionally to create a disconnect between the numbers on screen and a player's real-world wallet. As a result, the cost in real-world currency for any individual item is hidden, which once again distances players from the real cost of things. In addition, these currencies can only be purchased in pre-determined amounts. Hence players cannot simply buy the item and must always overpay. For instance, this tactic is present in the open-world action role-playing game Genshin Impact. If I wanted to get the trendy 'Red Dead of Night' Dilluc character skin worth 2480 crystals, I would be looking to spend my money on two options. The 3280 crystal bundle for $69 or the 1980 crystal bundle for $45 with a 980 crystal bundle for $22 to get a total of 2960 crystals for $67. Either way, no matter how you purchase the item, there will always be leftovers. Hence the psychology of the players would be to spend even more money in an attempt to use up the leftover currency from the previous purchase. But players do not realise that they will still have leftovers upon buying more currency and spending them. And so, the vicious cycle repeats as players sink more and more money into the game, not knowing that their 'leftovers' will never deplete to 'zero'.)
/E (When it comes to spending real-world money on games' IAPs, most games have a variety of thematically fitting currencies: crystals, coins, gems, points or credits et cetera. Currencies which are all designed intentionally to create a disconnect between the numbers on screen and a player's real-world wallet. As a result, the cost in real-world currency for any individual item is hidden, which once again distances players from the real cost of things. In addition, these currencies can only be purchased in pre-determined amounts. Hence players cannot simply buy the item and must always overpay. For instance, this tactic is present in the open-world action role-playing game Genshin Impact. If I wanted to get the trendy 'Red Dead of Night' Dilluc character skin worth 2480 crystals, I would be looking to spend my money on two options. The 3280 crystal bundle for $69 or the 1980 crystal bundle for $45 with a 980 crystal bundle for $22 to get a total of 2960 crystals for $67. Either way, no matter how you purchase the item, there will always be leftovers. Hence the psychology of the players would be to spend even more money in an attempt to use up the leftover currency from the previous purchase. But players do not realise that they will still have leftovers upon buying more currency and spending them. And so, the vicious cycle repeats as players sink more and more money into the game, not knowing that their 'leftovers' will never deplete to 'zero'.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
173 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Furthermore, the virtuality of the currency makes the player feel like they have not spent any physical cash at all. This makes it extremely easy for players to spend hundreds of thousands without the tangibility of lost money. The real-world equivalent of this is casino chips. Hypothetically, real notes or coins could be used to bet on a casino table, but casinos want patrons to forget that they are even spending money at all so they turn to poker chips instead. Therefore currency distortion is another tactic of predatory monetisation.)
/E (Furthermore, the virtuality of the currency makes the player feel like they have not spent any physical cash at all. This makes it extremely easy for players to spend hundreds of thousands without the tangibility of lost money. The real-world equivalent of this is casino chips. Hypothetically, real notes or coins could be used to bet on a casino table, but casinos want patrons to forget that they are even spending money at all so they turn to poker chips instead. Therefore currency distortion is another tactic of predatory monetisation.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
174 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Lastly, and most notoriously, is the use of pay-to-win mechanics. One of the primary reasons why players spend money on IAPs is so that they can 'stomp' or bully weaker players. Pay-to-win games often offer massive advantages to players who spend money on IAPs or currency, giving them an unfair superiority over players who do not. )
/E (Lastly, and most notoriously, is the use of pay-to-win mechanics. One of the primary reasons why players spend money on IAPs is so that they can 'stomp' or bully weaker players. Pay-to-win games often offer massive advantages to players who spend money on IAPs or currency, giving them an unfair superiority over players who do not. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
