186 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (That said, to address the elephant in the room, how do we stop it?)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
187 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Well, to avoid being one or to reduce the number of times we are a hypocrite, we must first see if there are any contradictions in our own jurisdiction and judgement. Being objective means there is no space for interpretations or exceptional cases and that facts remain facts regardless of who, where, what, why etc. Just like how regardless of the situation, abuse is still abuse, and murder is still murder. We should also accept that, in the end,  that imperfection is of human characteristic. Maturity is to admit and recognise our mistakes and then correct them. It can also be beneficial to picture ourselves in other people's situations. Acknowledge other people. Learn about your enemies. Take into account where they are coming from, and do not jump to conclusions.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
188 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (You may believe in morality and the 'right thing to do'. However, think about all the excuses when it comes to your own shortcomings. Is hypocrisy a defence mechanism? A way to project and or avoid responsibility against accusations from others? )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
189 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Many of us accuse the other party of doing the same thing, whether to impress someone else or bring somebody down. Ironically, when it comes to us, it's 'different'. In my opinion, this stems from the inability to be deferential \(also known as being aka humble\) along with our pride and ego. The reality of how things work makes living by our principles or upholding high moral standards difficult. We benefit greatly from people's positive opinions of us being kind and polite individuals, remaining entirely ignorant of any constrictions. The fear of accountability paired with the feeling of being morally superior to one is the driving force in most situations.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
190 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (To end it off, remember that, once again, nobody wants to deal with hypocrites. People are more likely to forgive you for admitting mistakes than refusing or dragging others down \(nobody likes snitches\). Don't be afraid of being human; authenticity is refreshing.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [9]
>>
